## Development

```bash
fvm install
fvm flutter pub get
make doctor
make verify
make run
```

{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}On macOS, `make macos-native-test` verifies the native lifecycle bridge used to
terminate an acknowledged secondary application instance before it enters
`runApp`.

{{end}}
`make run` builds the Go sidecar, exposes its path to Flutter, and starts the
current Flutter target. Desktop uses the Sidecar by default; selected Mobile and
Web targets use the HTTP backend:

```bash
make backend-serve
fvm flutter run -d chrome \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/rpc \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN=dev-token
```

The Bridra CLI can supervise the same development session through the project's
versioned framework dependency:

```bash
cd backend
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root ..
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root .. --device chrome
```

Mobile and custom devices require `--listen 0.0.0.0:<port>` plus an explicit
`--backend-url` reachable from the device.

{{if hasPlatform "android"}}With an Android Emulator already running, exercise the generated app and a real
temporary Go backend without Android hardware:

```bash
make android-emulator-smoke
```

The gate uses Android's `10.0.2.2` host alias and verifies Health, Greeting,
ordered Streaming/Progress, interrupted and resumed managed downloads/uploads,
a backend outage, and the complete flow again after reconnect. Debug and Profile
manifests allow local cleartext traffic; Release keeps Android's secure default.

{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "ios"}}On macOS with full Xcode, exercise the generated iOS app and real Go HTTP
backend in a Simulator or on a trusted physical iPhone:

```bash
make ios-simulator-smoke
make ios-device-smoke
```

The Simulator target verifies Health, Greeting, ordered Streaming/Progress, and
managed file transfers through the running iOS app. It interrupts a download
after 32 KiB and requires a byte-range resume while preserving known size,
SHA-256, and content. It also interrupts an upload at the same offset, requires
resume from that exact byte, and has Go consume and re-hash the completed upload.
Its Flutter phase stops after nine minutes total or four minutes without output.
Set `BRIDRA_IOS_SIMULATOR_DIAGNOSTICS_DIR` to preserve bounded failure evidence.
It is suitable for hosted macOS CI.

The device target selects the only available iPhone unless `DEVICE=<id>` is
provided, avoids occupied local ports, verifies Health and Greeting, forces a
backend outage and verifies reconnect, checks ordered Streaming and Progress
plus interrupted/resumed managed downloads and uploads before and after recovery,
and proves that a signed Profile app cold-launches twice without Flutter tooling.
Configure an Apple Development Team before running it and accept the one-time
Local Network prompt for a new bundle identifier; signing remains
application-owned.

{{end}}
## Builds

Build selected desktop Sidecar bundles or mobile/Web HTTP artifacts through the same local
Bridra CLI dependency:

```bash
{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}make macos-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "linux"}}make linux-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "windows"}}make windows-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "android"}}make android-build BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "ios"}}make ios-build BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "web"}}make web-build \
  BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc \
  BACKEND_TOKEN=replace-me
{{end}}
```

Set `BUILD_MODE=debug`, `profile`, or `release`. Profile and release HTTP builds
require an HTTPS `/rpc` endpoint and token. Each successful build writes a
token-free SHA-256 manifest under `build/bridra/`. iOS output is unsigned;
signing, notarization, installers, and store submission are separate release
steps.

## Contract generation

`schema/bridra.json` is the RPC source of truth. The project also owns
`schema/bridra.baseline.json`, an immutable snapshot of the reviewed deployed
contract:

```bash
make generate
make schema-check
make codegen-check
```

`make verify` runs the schema check automatically. A breaking wire change fails
unless the current application `protocolVersion` is greater than the baseline.
Neither `make generate` nor framework upgrades replace the baseline; update it
only after the new contract has been reviewed and deployed.

To compare against a different release or deployment explicitly:

```bash
cd backend
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra schema check \
  --against path/to/released-schema.json \
  --schema ../schema/bridra.json
```

Use `--json` for a machine-readable CI report.

Set `"stream": true` on a schema method to generate a typed
`Stream<RpcStreamEvent<ResultType>>`; use `framework.ProduceStream` in its Go
Controller.

Use a `"type": "file"` field for large input or output. Stage output through
`framework.FileTransferStoreKey`, then consume its generated `RpcFileReference`
with `BackendGateway.download`. Create input with `RpcFileUpload`, call
`BackendGateway.upload`, and consume it in Go with
`FileTransferStore.ConsumeUpload`. Desktop uses managed temporary files; HTTP
uses short-lived capabilities with byte-range retry. Both paths verify size and
SHA-256.

